### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim that "Joe Biden wants to ban fracking" is based on cherry-picked statements from his 2019 and early 2020 Democratic primary debates—including a July 2019 remark about eliminating fossil fuels and a March 2020 comment about "no new fracking"—but it ignores substantial and critical context that significantly changes the picture. During the general election, Biden explicitly and repeatedly stated, "I am not banning fracking. Let me say that again. I am not banning fracking," as reported by [Energy Now](https://energynow.com/2020/09/the-biden-flip-flop-u-s-presidential-hopeful-biden-now-says-he-would-not-ban-fracking/) and [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/23/politics/biden-fracking-fact-check/index.html), and his actual 2020 campaign platform focused on measures like methane pollution limits and restrictions on new federal-land drilling rather than a complete ban. Furthermore, as [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-10-21/here-s-why-biden-couldn-t-ban-fracking-but-could-restrict-it) noted, a president lacks the legal authority to unilaterally ban fracking on private and state lands, making the claim not only selectively sourced—per [AP News](https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-joe-biden-donald-trump-pennsylvania-elections-c28fbf9d921915f3a65c01e6c4cebcf6)—but also misleading about both Biden's actual policy positions and presidential powers.